\details{
\strong{Round trip.} For a data frame \code{x} that \code{\link[dtaparser:read_dta]{dtaparser::read_dta()}}
returned, \code{read_parquet()} of the file gives a tibble \code{identical()} to
\code{x}.

\strong{Numbers.} Integers are INT32 and doubles DOUBLE; a \code{storage}
attribute (\code{"byte"}, \code{"int"}, \code{"long"}, \code{"float"}, \code{"double"}), as
dtatools' columns have, writes that type instead, and a value it cannot
hold is an error. \code{NA} is a null. \code{.a} to \code{.z} (\code{\link[dtaparser:tagged_na]{dtaparser::tagged_na()}})
are written as tagged NaNs, the bits R holds them in, so a column that
holds any is DOUBLE; other tools read them as NaN, and arrow and
nanoparquet as tagged NAs. \code{missing_letters = FALSE} writes them as
nulls instead, losing the letters. NaN is written as NaN.

\strong{Value labels.} A column's \code{labels} attribute, as a
\code{\link[dtaparser:with_labels]{dtaparser_labelled}} or haven_labelled
column has, is written as a value label set named by its \code{label_set}
attribute or else the column's name. Columns sharing a set must have
the same labels.

\strong{Dates and times.} A \code{Date} is a DATE; a \code{POSIXct} a TIMESTAMP in
microseconds, adjusted to UTC, with its \code{tzone} kept. A \code{Date} with
fractions of a day, and a \code{POSIXct} whose seconds no whole number of
microseconds gives back exactly, are written as DOUBLE days or seconds
since 1970, so that they read back unchanged; other tools then see
numbers.

\strong{Factors} are written dictionary-encoded, every level in order in
every row group, so arrow, pyarrow and polars read them as factors and
categoricals too. \strong{Logicals} are BOOLEAN. \strong{Character} columns are
text of any length (strings must be UTF-8; see \code{\link[=enc2utf8]{enc2utf8()}}). \strong{A list
of raw vectors} (or \code{NULL}) is binary. bit64's \strong{integer64} is INT64.
Any other column type (another list, a nested data frame, complex
numbers, \code{POSIXlt}, \code{difftime}) is an error naming the column.

\strong{Metadata.} A column's \code{label} attribute is its variable label and
its \code{format} its display format. The data frame's attributes give the
rest: \code{label}, \code{notes}, \code{characteristics} (a data frame with character
columns \code{variable}, \code{name}, \code{value}) and \code{sort_order} (column names), as
for \code{save_dta()}.
